    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The debate over whether the Roman imperial apparatus could consciously implement policy to pursue long-term security aims in the form of grand strategy has gripped Classicists and military historians for nearly fifty years. This thesis argues that the concept of grand strategy provides a useful conceptual framework for understanding the Roman Empire’s security policies beyond the whims of individual emperors and reactive military action. To systematically analyze Roman grand strategy in the East, this thesis integrates recent insights from the field of grand strategic studies to develop a methodology examining the intentional allocation of legions and emperors’ policies towards Armenia. Armenia’s critical position between Rome and its greatest rival states, the Parthian and Sasanian Empires, renders the kingdom a useful focal point of analysis for Rome’s security agenda on its Eastern frontier. This analysis reveals that in the early decades of the empire, Rome prioritized the stability and security of its Eastern territories by\r\nactively counterbalancing Parthia in Armenia. Over the course of the second century and beyond, Roman grand strategy increasingly elevated expansion in the East as a chief grand strategic priority, transforming Armenia’s role as the centerpiece of a balance of power order with Parthia into a gateway for Roman expansion into Mesopotamia.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
